Implemented Vercel Speed Insights for Next.js Project

I successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights following the official documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart).

## Changes Made:

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` package (version automatically resolved by npm)
- Package added to dependencies in `package.json`
- Updated `package-lock.json` to reflect new dependency

### 2. Code Integration
**Modified: `app/layout.tsx`**
- Added import statement: `import { SpeedInsights } from '@vercel/speed-insights/next';`
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component before the closing `</body>` tag in the RootLayout component
- This placement ensures Speed Insights is loaded on all pages of the application

## Implementation Details:

The integration follows Next.js App Router best practices:
- Used the Next.js-specific import path `@vercel/speed-insights/next`
- Placed the component in the root layout to ensure it's included site-wide
- No additional configuration needed; the component works automatically once deployed to Vercel

## Verification:

✅ Build completed successfully (`npm run build`)
✅ No ESLint warnings or errors (`npm run lint`)
✅ All existing functionality preserved
✅ Lock file updated for dependency consistency

## Next Steps:

To see Speed Insights in action:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel (production deployment)
2. Visit the Vercel dashboard and navigate to Speed Insights
3. Click "Enable" if not already enabled
4. Once users visit your site, performance data will start appearing in the dashboard

The Speed Insights component will automatically collect Core Web Vitals and performance metrics from real user visits.
# Vercel Speed Insights Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for the YoYo Map project following the latest official documentation.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` package using npm
- Updated `package.json` with the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with resolved dependency tree

### 2. Component Integration (app/layout.tsx)
- Added import statement: `import { SpeedInsights } from '@vercel/speed-insights/next';`
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component in the root layout next to the existing `<Analytics />` component
- Follows the App Router pattern for Next.js 13.5+ as documented in the official Vercel quickstart guide

### 3. Content Security Policy Updates (next.config.js)
- Added `https://va.vercel-scripts.com` to the `script-src` CSP directive
- Added `https://vitals.vercel-insights.com` to the `connect-src` CSP directive
- These domains are required for Speed Insights to function properly and send performance metrics

## Implementation Details

- Framework: Next.js 15.5.15 with App Router
- Package Manager: npm (detected from existing package-lock.json)
- Installation followed official Vercel documentation fetched from: https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart
- CSP requirements based on community feedback from Speed Insights GitHub repository

## Testing & Verification

✅ Build completed successfully with no errors
✅ Linter passed with no warnings or errors
✅ All lock files properly updated
✅ Existing functionality preserved (Analytics component remains intact)

## Next Steps

To enable Speed Insights data collection:
1. Deploy this update to Vercel
2. Navigate to the Vercel dashboard
3. Select "Speed Insights" in the sidebar
4. Choose the project and click "Enable"
5. After deployment and user visits, performance metrics will appear in the dashboard

The Speed Insights component is now installed and will automatically collect Core Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS, FCP, TTFB, INP) once enabled in the Vercel dashboard.
